# Code-block minification

@lede
Fenced code blocks compile twice. The HTML target keeps your formatting.
The LLM target strips whitespace and comments where it is safe to.
@end

## Supported languages

@t
| Languages | Status
| `json`, `jsonl` | Minified
| `js`, `ts`, `rust`, `c`, `cpp`, `java`, `go`, `sql` | Minified
| `python`, `yaml`, `makefile` | Permanently refused

Refused languages have significant whitespace. Forcing minification on
them via `@minify` or by adding the tag to `minify_languages` is @ec[B0704]
*"language uses significant whitespace and cannot be safely minified"* —
the compiler emits the error and falls back to verbatim.

## The three states

A fenced block can be in one of three minification states. The state is
set by an attribute after the language tag.

State 1 — *full minification* (default for languages in the allowlist).
The block strips all whitespace and all comments.

State 2 — *verbatim*. Add `@nominify` after the language tag. The block
is emitted unchanged.

State 3 — *strip whitespace, keep comments*. Add `@minify-keep-comments`
after the language tag. Comments survive; whitespace collapses to the
minimum required to preserve token boundaries.

## A worked example

Source:

```rust
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    // Adds two numbers.
    a + b
}
```

LLM-mode output (State 1, full minification):

```text
fn add(a:i32,b:i32)->i32{a+b}
```

Same source with `@minify-keep-comments` (State 3):

```text
fn add(a:i32,b:i32)->i32{/* Adds two numbers.*/a+b}
```

The conversion from `//` to `/* */` triggers @ec[B0703] *"line comment
converted to block-comment form for minification"* per converted line.
Silence with `--allow B0703` if you have manually verified the line
comments do not contain `*/`.

## Per-block opt-in

```rust @minify
{
  "force_minify": true
}
```

`@minify` overrides `compile.llm.minify_code_blocks = false` and the
document-level `minify_code = false` from frontmatter.

## Per-block opt-out

```rust @nominify
fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
    // Adds two numbers.
    a + b
}
```

Use `@nominify` when the formatting itself is the lesson — pretty-printed
JSON in a "how to format your config" page, indented Rust in a "writing
readable code" guide. The block is emitted exactly as written.

## Per-language preservation rules

Each minifier respects its language's lexical structure:

@dl
JS / TS
: Newlines preserved; ASI behavior would otherwise change. The minifier
  inserts an explicit `;` before stripping a newline that would have
  terminated a statement.
Go
: Same as JS / TS. Lexical newlines drive automatic semicolon insertion.
C / C++
: Newlines around `#`-preprocessor lines are preserved. Collapsing them
  breaks the preprocessor.
Rust
: Raw strings (`r#"..."#`, with arbitrary `#` counts), byte strings, and
  the fact that block comments *nest* are all handled.
Java
: Block comments do *not* nest. Annotations (`@Override`, etc.) are
  tokens, not comments — preserved.
SQL
: Whitespace stripped. Keyword case is left alone.
JSON / JSONL
: Single-line for JSON. JSONL minifies each line independently; empty
  lines and lines that fail to parse fall back to verbatim with a
  warning.
@end

## Correctness

Minification is *semantically lossless*. Each minifier passes a property
test:

```text
parse → minify → re-parse  ⇒  AST equal
```

If a minifier cannot guarantee this for a given input, it falls back to
verbatim and emits @ec[B0701]. Emitting silently broken code to an LLM is
worse than emitting verbose code.

## Reference-destruction warning

A fenced block longer than 50 source lines that gets minified emits
@ec[B0702] *"minified code block was originally many lines; LLM consumers
cannot reference specific lines"* once.

This is a warning, not an error. Suppress with `--allow B0702` if your
prompt does not depend on line references inside code samples. Keep it on
if your prompt asks the LLM to "look at line 12" — minification deletes
that addressability.

## Configuration knobs

`brief.toml`:

```toml
[compile.llm]
minify_code_blocks = true
minify_languages = ["json", "jsonl", "rust", "go"]
preserve_code_fences = true
```

@dl
minify_code_blocks
: Master switch. Default true. When false, all blocks are verbatim.
minify_languages
: Allowlist. The compiler refuses to minify languages not in this list,
  even if a built-in minifier exists for them. Lets users gate which
  languages are minified per project.
preserve_code_fences
: Default true. When false, fences are dropped from minified blocks in
  LLM output. Saves a few tokens per block; loses the structural cue
  that the LLM is looking at code.
@end

## Cost savings

Empirical measurements on the project's own corpus:

@t
| Document shape | Token reduction (LLM mode vs. Markdown)
| Prose-heavy | around 20 percent
| Mixed prose + tables | around 25 percent
| JSON-heavy | over 50 percent

JSON-heavy documents get the largest win because Brief is the only Markdown
replacement that ships JSON minification by default. The reduction is
durable: the Markdown ecosystem cannot ship this without breaking changes
to renderer behavior.
